{
  "case_id": "2007-06-6911",
  "document_hash": "2e9c42eb6cfd922568f786a3e13fce2a1d560c93ef0723da8e9006855e5fc8f7",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Richard A. Coad",
      "case_id": "2007-06-6911",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2e9c42eb6cfd922568f786a3e13fce2a1d560c93ef0723da8e9006855e5fc8f7",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2e9c42eb6cfd-0000-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The provision at issue determines which convictions constitute a prior felony for imposition of the Federal firearms ban and its increased penalties. Its exemption clause prescribes certain ways in which a conviction that otherwise meets that definition is nevertheless exempt. Specifically, it looks to a State's indication that an offense is deprived of any continuing effect, such as whether the status of an offender's civil rights are the same after a conviction as they were before conviction."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2007-06-6911",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2e9c42eb6cfd922568f786a3e13fce2a1d560c93ef0723da8e9006855e5fc8f7",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2e9c42eb6cfd-0000-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GINSBURG: So if you had a statute -- a State like, I'm told, Vermont, that doesn't take away any one's civil rights, not even a first degree murderer's, then that first degree murderer would be equated to someone whose civil rights were taken away and then restored."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Richard A. Coad",
      "case_id": "2007-06-6911",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2e9c42eb6cfd922568f786a3e13fce2a1d560c93ef0723da8e9006855e5fc8f7",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2e9c42eb6cfd-0000-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. COAD: That's correct. And I think that the Government points to Maine as an example where no offenders lose their civil rights, but felons lose their gun rights and certain misdemeanants also lose their gun rights, but get that gun right back. And in States like Maine and in Vermont, the \"unless\" clause still applies and still precludes those types of convictions."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2007-06-6911",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2e9c42eb6cfd922568f786a3e13fce2a1d560c93ef0723da8e9006855e5fc8f7",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2e9c42eb6cfd-0000-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 5,
        "start": 5
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SCALIA: Mr. Coad, how are -- how are civil rights which have been taken away typically restored? What is -- what is the process for restoring them? Just if you don't commit another offense within a certain number of years?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Richard A. Coad",
      "case_id": "2007-06-6911",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2e9c42eb6cfd922568f786a3e13fce2a1d560c93ef0723da8e9006855e5fc8f7",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2e9c42eb6cfd-0000-0004",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 5,
        "start": 5
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. COAD: It depends on the State. In the NACDL lodging that the Court has -- I believe 29 States were identified as having some type of restoration procedures. The majority of those, it is by automatic operation of law. So there's no subsequent conditions met by the offender."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2007-06-6911",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2e9c42eb6cfd922568f786a3e13fce2a1d560c93ef0723da8e9006855e5fc8f7",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2e9c42eb6cfd-0000-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 9,
        "start": 9
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE ROBERTS: So your argument, if accepted, would essentially weed out, or at least for a majority of the States, weed out subdivision (a)(20)(A)(b) -- (a)(20)(B)? In other words, there's coverage under the statute if you are convicted under a misdemeanor and you want to say, well, if your civil rights were never taken away, you shouldn't be covered."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Richard A. Coad",
      "case_id": "2007-06-6911",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2e9c42eb6cfd922568f786a3e13fce2a1d560c93ef0723da8e9006855e5fc8f7",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2e9c42eb6cfd-0000-0006",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 15,
        "start": 15
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. COAD: I think it is incorrect in the sense that when the government sort of narrowed the number of States that we identified as being problem States, what it ignores is that the Armed Career Criminal Act and the prohibition against firearms goes back forever really. You have to look at all of State law for 1980s, '70s '60s."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Coad",
      "case_id": "2007-06-6911",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2e9c42eb6cfd922568f786a3e13fce2a1d560c93ef0723da8e9006855e5fc8f7",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2e9c42eb6cfd-0001-0004",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 22,
        "start": 22
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "In North Dakota the State has decided that if a felon is not sentenced to imprisonment, then the felon retains his civil rights, and his right to possess a firearm is restored after a short waiting period."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kennedy",
      "case_id": "2007-06-6911",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2e9c42eb6cfd922568f786a3e13fce2a1d560c93ef0723da8e9006855e5fc8f7",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2e9c42eb6cfd-0001-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 23,
        "start": 23
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KENNEDY: Do you know, since you seem to have looked at it: In North Carolina, for the person whose had his civil rights taken away, is -- is there any mechanism to get them back earlier by -- by applying for clemency or a pardon or --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Joseffer",
      "case_id": "2007-06-6911",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2e9c42eb6cfd922568f786a3e13fce2a1d560c93ef0723da8e9006855e5fc8f7",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2e9c42eb6cfd-0001-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 25,
        "start": 25
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "We just have a two points. The first is that restoring a right does not mean leaving it alone, and the second is that the statute is not absurd."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2007-06-6911",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2e9c42eb6cfd922568f786a3e13fce2a1d560c93ef0723da8e9006855e5fc8f7",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2e9c42eb6cfd-0001-0003",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 37,
        "start": 37
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "(Whereupon, at 11:42 a.m., the case in the above-entitled matter was submitted.)"
    },
